- Finding a phone: from obsession to purpose, point graph (source: 11_finding_a_phone/source.txt)
    Summary: Seeking a phone that solves problems without ecosystems or gimmicks, from Droid obsession to rugged Ulefone and a data-driven search. (¶1, ¶16)
        - 1 Theme: Early phone history: simple reliability, smartphone immersion, then disillusion and retreat. (¶2, ¶3, ¶4, ¶5, ¶6)
            - 1.1 The first phone, a Sony Ericsson J100, was no-frills, reliable, durable, and hardy; it never let him down. (¶2)
            - 1.2 After a phoneless spell, he bought the Motorola Droid on day one; it quickly became a centerpiece of his life. (¶3)
            - 1.3 The Droid was one of the first Android smartphones widely available in the U.S., and it ushered in a new era. (¶3, ¶4)
            - 1.4 He explored every feature, tried one of the world's first smartwatches, and was captivated; his phone was tool, organizer, and companion. (¶4)
            - 1.5 The honeymoon didn't last: notifications, endless customization, and attention-stealing apps made him feel more consumer than user. (¶5)
            - 1.6 He made a drastic decision and switched to a dial-pad phone. (¶6)
        - 2 Theme: Needs redefined: offline functionality, toughness, and independence over flashy features. (¶7, ¶8, ¶9, ¶10, ¶11, ¶12)
            - 2.1 He tested small and large screens, physical keyboards, stylus-equipped phones, and the latest smartwatches. (¶7)
            - 2.2 No matter what he tried, phones were not built for people like him: they sold unneeded features, pushed unwanted ads, locked him into avoided ecosystems. (¶8)
            - 2.3 That realization shifted his focus to phones prioritizing functionality over flashiness. (¶9)
            - 2.4 Priorities: long battery life, waterproofing and shockproofing, SD card slots and headphone jacks, decent mic and speaker, customization. (¶10)
            - 2.5 Didn't care about the best camera, AI photo 'enhancements', or cloud storage; preferred offline independence. (¶11)
            - 2.6 Apple and Google deliberately limited offline options, removing SD card slots and offering minimal onboard storage to push cloud services. (¶12)
            - 2.7 He wanted to take the phone to the mountains for music, reading, and notes without worrying about connectivity. (¶12)
        - 3 Theme: Ulefone delivered rugged value: toughness, battery, price, and survival. (¶13, ¶14, ¶15)
            - 3.1 Ulefone devices were a revelation: a small Chinese company specializing in rugged phones, not competing with Apple or Samsung but solving real problems. (¶13)
            - 3.2 Loved the durability (waterproof, shockproof, dustproof) and practical features: dual SIM, SD card slots, headphone jacks. (¶14)
            - 3.3 One Ulefone model lasted an entire week on a single charge. (¶14)
            - 3.4 These phones cost about 1/10th the price of flagship devices from major brands. (¶14)
            - 3.5 Evidence: Put through hell: countless drops, swimming, abuse that would destroy most devices; they held up remarkably well. (¶15)
            - 3.6 Evidence: A dive a bit too deep into a lake this summer made the current Ulefone act up, but it still works. (¶15)
        - 4 Theme: Why this matters: phones as tools, with smaller makers filling the gap. (¶16, ¶17, ¶18, ¶19)
            - 4.1 Phones are tools, not status symbols; he doesn't want a device that decides what's important or forces conformity. (¶16)
            - 4.2 He wants a phone that fits his life, solves his problems, and respects his choices. (¶16)
            - 4.3 Big players (Apple, Google, Samsung) don't cater to people like him; their ecosystems prioritize profit over genuine user needs. (¶17)
            - 4.4 Smaller companies like Ulefone are filling that gap. (¶17)
            - 4.5 You don't have to settle for what the market says you need; searching can find tools that work for you, not against you. (¶18)
            - 4.6 His journey was about rejecting trends and seeking solutions; the world chases megapixels and AI gimmicks while he keeps a rugged, reliable phone. (¶19)
        - 5 Theme: An abandoned Pixel deal led to an LLM-assisted search and comparison pipeline. (¶20)
            - 5.1 Theme: Pixel detour: ordering on a deal, anxiety, then walking away. (¶20)
                - 5.1.1 Ordered a latest Google Pixel on a Black Friday deal, thinking to maybe try the always-connected life; people generally said good things. (¶20)
                - 5.1.2 While waiting he grew anxious: multi-year lock-in then remainder payment, for a phone he wouldn't enjoy and that would limit his choices. (¶20)
                - 5.1.3 He hadn't paid; after extra charges for random things appeared, he declined them and got back on the market. (¶20)
            - 5.2 Theme: Pipeline: O1 suggestions, GSMArena specs, scraper and cleaner code. (¶20, ¶21, ¶28)
                - 5.2.1 Friends' recommendations weren't enough; asked OpenAI's O1 for smaller-company phones matching his 5G, eSIM, SD, battery, waterproof, and camera wants. (¶20)
                - 5.2.2 O1 gave a bunch of phones with generally wrong specs but correct model names. (¶20)
                - 5.2.3 He found specs on gsmarena.com, but comparing was hard, so asked gpt-4o-mini to write scraping code. (¶20)
                - 5.2.4 The scraper rotates user agents, retries 3 times with backoff, sleeps 2 to 5 seconds, and parses GSMArena specs into a DataFrame. (¶22, ¶23, ¶24)
                - 5.2.5 Gave it 18 GSMArena URLs, including a duplicated realme Narzo 60 entry, and saved the table to CSV. (¶25, ¶26)
                - 5.2.6 Wished websites existed to do this analysis simply without scraping them. (¶27)
                - 5.2.7 Asked Claude 3.5 Sonnet to clean the table; it first ignored the actual columns, then mostly worked after more prompts. (¶28)
                - 5.2.8 Caveat: unchecked; he assumes it made several errors but calls it a good starting point. (¶28)
                - 5.2.9 The cleaner extracts brand, model, 5G, eSIM, release year, screen size, dual SIM, battery mAh, charging speed, SD slot, cameras, and price. (¶29, ¶30, ¶31, ¶32)
                - 5.2.10 BeautifulSoup misses a lot of details; pandas read_html reads this site well; request volume and proxies need managing next. (¶41)
        - 6 Theme: Filter outcomes: two matches, a returned trial, and the next candidate. (¶33, ¶39, ¶42, ¶43)
            - 6.1 Theme: Results and gaps: two matches, Fairphone too old, Edge 40 missing. (¶34, ¶39, ¶40)
                - 6.1.1 The results table has 18 rows (duplicated Narzo 60), release years 2021 to 2024, and many missing prices. (¶33, ¶34, ¶35, ¶36, ¶37)
                - 6.1.2 Only Fairphone 4 (2021) and Moto G Power (2024) have 5G, eSIM, and an SD slot; cameras aren't great, screens too big. (¶38, ¶39)
                - 6.1.3 Fairphone 4 seems a bit too old (2021); he might have to expand the search a little. (¶39)
                - 6.1.4 Wanted to compare the Motorola Edge 40 but it didn't show up; it seems to lack an SD card slot. (¶40)
            - 6.2 Theme: TCL 50 XE trial failed; Motorola G85 next. (¶42, ¶43)
                - 6.2.1 TCL 50 XE NXTPAPER returned after a couple of weeks: weak performance, uncomfortably hot video calls, so-so camera. (¶42)
                - 6.2.2 The TCL screen was not at all readable in direct light, even lamp light, worse than regular screens where tilting helps. (¶42)
                - 6.2.3 Paper and ink color modes were good ideas but restricted and not customizable; the ink mode was just black-and-white, not e-ink as advertised. (¶42)
                - 6.2.4 Next: Motorola G85, water repellent design only; wanted the more waterproof IP68 G75 but couldn't find it in stores. (¶43)
                - 6.2.5 Motorola generally isn't supposed to have a good camera, so he won't expect much there. (¶43)
